<h1>Orcraft Test Group</h1>
<p>A group collects related tests. Unlike a test, a group has no body: it is declared, not coded. Its
  config names the tests it holds, and the framework does the rest.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTestGroup,
	OrcraftTestGroupConfig,
	OrcraftTestGroupResult,
	OrcraftConfigField,
)

from my_group.my_first_test.my_first_test import MyFirstTest
from my_group.my_second_test.my_second_test import MySecondTest


class MyGroupConfig(OrcraftTestGroupConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Group")
	description = OrcraftConfigField.Text(
		default="Groups the tests that cover one area of the system under test."
	)
	tests = OrcraftConfigField.Tests(
		default_factory=lambda: [
			MyFirstTest(),
			MySecondTest(),
		]
	)


class MyGroupResult(OrcraftTestGroupResult):
	pass


class MyGroup(OrcraftTestGroup):
	def __init__(self) -> None:
		super().__init__(config=MyGroupConfig())
		self.result = MyGroupResult()


def main() -> None:
	MyGroup().run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}

<h2 id="tests-field">The tests field</h2>
<p><code>tests</code> uses <code>default_factory</code> rather than <code>default</code> because it
  constructs objects. A factory is called once per config instance, so two group instances never end up
  sharing the same test objects - a mistake that a plain <code>default</code> list would make easy and
  hard to spot.</p>

<p>The result class is usually empty. Subclassing <code>OrcraftTestGroupResult</code> without adding
  anything is still worth doing: it gives the group a result type of its own to extend later, and keeps
  the three-class shape consistent across the suite.</p>

<h2 id="why-group">What a group buys you</h2>
<p>A group is the unit the controller lets you act on as a whole:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Configure once, apply to many.</strong> Setting a parameter at group level applies it to
    every test in the group, so a shared executable folder or endpoint is entered once rather than per
    test.</li>
  <li><strong>Run a slice.</strong> A group can be run without the rest of the suite, which is how a
    focused rerun stays focused.</li>
  <li><strong>Aggregate reporting.</strong> Group results carry both test counters and case counters, so
    a group answers how many of its tests came back clean and how many cases that took.</li>
</ul>

<p>The tutorial works through the first two in
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/running-a-group/">Running a group</a>.</p>

<h2 id="config-fields">Group config fields</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>name</code></td>
      <td><code>"Test Group"</code></td>
      <td>Display name in the GUI, CLI and logs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>description</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Free text describing what the group covers.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>version</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Group version, recorded with the results.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>tests</code></td>
      <td><code>[]</code></td>
      <td>The tests this group runs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>parallel</code></td>
      <td><code>True</code></td>
      <td>Whether the group's tests may run concurrently.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>upload_test_package</code></td>
      <td><code>"if_missing"</code></td>
      <td>Whether the test package is uploaded to the endpoint before the run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>update_endpoint_app</code></td>
      <td><code>"defer"</code></td>
      <td>Whether the endpoint application is updated before the run.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="parallel">Parallel execution</h2>
<p>With <code>parallel</code> left at its default, tests in a group may run concurrently, which is what
  makes a suite spread across several endpoints finish in a useful amount of time. Set it to
  <code>False</code> for a group whose tests contend for something the framework cannot see - a shared
  device, a fixed port, a single working directory.</p>

<p>Note that endpoint contention is already handled: an endpoint is held for the duration of a run, and
  another run wanting it waits, unless the endpoint field is declared
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#endpoint-options"><code>shared</code></a>. Turning
  off parallelism is for the contention Orcraft does not know about.</p>

<h2 id="defer">Policies that flow downward</h2>
<p><code>update_endpoint_app</code> is the one field where a group deliberately does not decide by
  default. <code>defer</code> means the group expresses no opinion and each test keeps its own choice;
  any other value overrides every test beneath it.</p>

<p>That distinction matters when a suite mixes tests that must run against a pinned endpoint version with
  tests that always want the latest. Leaving the group at <code>defer</code> preserves both intents;
  setting a concrete value is an explicit decision to override them.</p>

<p><code>update_endpoint_app</code> takes one of four values at group level:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Update the endpoint application before every run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>on_mismatch</code></td>
      <td>Update only when the endpoint application does not match the controller. The default for a test.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Leave the endpoint application as it is.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>defer</code></td>
      <td>Express no opinion and leave the decision to the level below. The default for a group.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><code>upload_test_package</code> has no <code>defer</code>: a group always states a policy, and it
  applies to every test beneath it.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Upload on every run. The safe choice while the suite is changing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>if_missing</code></td>
      <td>Upload only when the endpoint does not already have the package. The default.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Never upload - the endpoint is expected to have the package already.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="standalone">Running a group</h2>
<p>Like a test, a group runs on its own:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('def main() -> None:
	MyGroup().run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}

<p>This runs every test in the group, in order or in parallel according to <code>parallel</code>, and
  logs a group summary after the individual test results. Once a group is doing what you want, add it to
  a <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/suites/">suite</a>.</p>
